description:
FILED s521c (Opus 5.5, 2026-10-18) from the #1576 one-file acceptance run of Path-Tiny t/filesystem.t (subtests 'iterator' rows 39 + 47 -- `while ( my $file = $iter->() ) { push @contents, $file }` collects empties) and the 'visit' rows 40 + 48 (same file, probably the same mechanism through `$_[0]` -- confirm when fixed).

SILENT WRONG: a REFERENCE assigned to a loop-head `my` and pushed onto an array is lost -- the array ends up holding the loop variable's LAST value (the false one that ended the loop); a plain string survives.

REPRO (probed, perl 5.40.3 vs PCL on 68aeebf5 + s521c, scratch/s521c/pr/whilemy.pl):
    package P; use overload '""' => sub { $_[0][0] }, bool => sub { 1 }, fallback => 1;
    package main;
    my @o = map { bless [$_], 'P' } qw(x y);
    my $j = 0; my $it2 = sub { return if $j > $#o; return $o[$j++] };
    my @d; while (my $x = $it2->()) { push @d, $x }
    print "obj=@d n=", scalar(@d), "\n";
perl: obj=x y n=2      PCL: obj=  n=2      (with plain strings 'a','b','c' PCL is right)
Area: #297 / #300 (loop-head `my`), the push of a ref-holding box -- is the BOX pushed (an alias) instead of its value?  Shrink further (unblessed [1] refs) when taking it.
REACH: Path-Tiny filesystem.t rows 39, 40, 47, 48; any iterator idiom over objects.
